it started like a routine business trip  and  ended in china's secret detention system  stanley wong was crossing the border to  mainland china  when he was stopped border police  searched his phone and questioned him  for several  hours then  in the evening two police officers came  they handcuffed me and put a black hood  on my head  then they took me away  for fear of retribution we cannot show  his face  stanley wong is not his real name his  accounts cannot be verified  independently  but his story is in line with accounts  from other detainees  at that time border police had begun  systematically researching the phones of  hong kongers  crossing the border between hong kong  and the mainland  a photo of wong attending a peaceful  march in june  caught their attention he explained it  was an authorized march and that hong  kong's laws allowed him to participate  but they wouldn't listen  they said this was about a matter of  national security  and that it wasn't up to me to decide  what endangering it means  national security is above all other  laws when national security is concerned  they said  we can catch you in hong kong or any  other place in the world  huang was held in a single cell that had  been covered in soft material  the glass door was translucent from the  outside only  lights left permanently on over and over  they interrogated him  asking about photos on his phone and  contacts to foreign nationals  i had to crouch like this for the whole  duration of the interrogation  they placed me right under an air  conditioner with the wind blowing at me  constantly  each interrogation lasted a few hours  and whenever they thought i didn't sit  well  they would beat me like this  punch my back or kick me in the legs  the number of people who've been  detained at the border remains unknown  wong says he was able to catch glimpses  into other cells  that helped people whom he believes were  fellow hong kongers caught at the border  after 36 hours he was released  my family tried to ask the public  security where i was  but they just wouldn't say anything the  police even denied they knew me  and that i had crossed the border i  understood that i could easily be  disappeared anytime  he's dealt with the experience quite  well he says  and he won't let it stop him but now  when he crosses a border  his heart starts to beat a little faster  let's bring in the reporter now who  filed that for sdws matthias  berlinga joining us from beijing good  day to you  matthias matthias is there any way to  know  how widespread this violent form of  intimidation  is right now  it's very hard to say what we know is  that especially  in august there was a campaign to  intimidate hong kongers  at the border by searching their phones  and questioning them  most people were stopped at the border  they were questioned at the border  and then let go after a few hours these  cases  like the mans that we just saw  where people were taken away into uh  secret prisons or or interrogation  facilities uh are much rarer what we  know  is for of course the case of one  british consulate employee simon chung  who was held there for 15 days  and who has just given an account of  torture  that was much worse uh than what our man  here  describes okay so we're not only talking  about  torture though we're also talking about  as stanley  wong expressed one of his fears of being  disappeared  now now what is being disappeared and  how much  of a threat is that for hong kong  protesters  well people can be disappeared in china  at any time  they will be taken into some facilities  that are especially run  we know from mainland chinese  disappeared that they can be held  up to several months even more than a  year in these  detention facilities without their  relatives being  noticed i am not aware of any case of  uh people during this protest movement  having been disappeared  in this way what we hear of or what we  know of  is that there have been allegations  earlier one hong kong  bookseller disappeared for several  months in a chinese  detention center and he says he was  kidnapped from hong kong streets  okay this is a brutal toolkit if we  could call that  that the chinese authorities are putting  together how effective  is intimidation right now for the  protesters in hong kong  the effect that intimidation has had so  far  is to stir up people's anger we are  seeing several tactics of  intimidation these border controls that  by the way applied  also to journalists although no  journalist was being detained at the  border  there were just one set the other set is  to ramp  up uh the the the police uh  action and the police brutality also the  weaponry of the police to  crack down harder on protesters what  we're seeing this week this this  siege of the university that that some  protesters had occupied  is of course a tactics that has little  in common with  riot control and more in common with  warfare  or combating terrorists who have  occupied or taken hostage of  a certain place so all these tactics  have not been able  to keep people off the streets and they  have  contributed their part in making the  radicals more radical  and escalating violence so it is quite  unclear what china  is aiming to achieve with these tactics  at the moment  matthias billingham thanks very much for  that report and for  our talk this morning from beijing
 

